bles uh yet we're a long  way off that we we did hear however  about 24 hours ago from the german  chancellor who called this political  blackmail  well it is to the extent that now uh  europe cannot really sanction gazprom  bank  and therefore they cannot cut russia off  from the global financial system because  if they were to sanction gas from bank  then it means that germany or other  european countries could not pay for  their gas uh and and therefore they  would disrupt the contract and lead to  gas disruption so to that extent it does  raise the stakes  uh and it and it means that you know  europe pretty much has to now kind of  treat gas from banks separately  otherwise they they could run the risk  of gas disruption so it does raise the  stakes but it's it's it is not what we  originally were told which is that  customers would have to pay  rubles into gazprom today that's not the  case they pay their foreign exchange  into gas from bank who then does  conversion for them it makes gas from  bank more important  for the russian payment system and also  for europe's energy security understood  so just on the issue though of gas  disruption i mean there there had been  some speculation up to this point that  um could russia actually turn off the  taps to europe is that likely at this  point  i would say no not at all because  remember russia is just as dependent on  the money it receives for exporting the  gas as the european customers are for  their energy needs so this will be a  mutually destructive move by russia and  there's no indication that russia is  planning that they are certainly playing  clever as it were with regard to the  payment system to try and protect casper  and bank to try and protect uh the one  russian bank in the swift system  but there is no indication whatsoever  that russia is planning on disrupting  export flows uh despite everything  that's happening at a political level  and ukraine russia has been consistently  has been very consistent in this  position that it would always honor  foreign contracts provided the  counterparty does the same so russia  says it will not be the one to break the  contract it is kind of tailoring the  contracts but it said it will never  break it so there's no indication  whatsoever that russia is planning to  cut off any uh energy supplies or other  material supplies to the west so long as  they can get paid for those exports and  just looking at sort of the bigger  picture and the price of crude oil it  has been inflationary over the past  couple of months as you know  uh how do how much pressure do you think  there is on other oil producing nations  uh to turn up their their oil  productions  well clearly there's a lot of pressure  from the consuming countries to to do so  on opec countries in particular but do  bear in mind that the opec plus uh group  has been struggling to actually meet  their production targets anyway they  have not  they took a long time for example to  raise production to the january agreed  levels and since then have been falling  uh well short now of course countries  like saudi arabia and the united  emirates do have spare capacity but they  seem unwilling to move too quickly  because remember they're now making uh  finally making a lot of money uh  running budget surplus  based on the current oil price uh those  countries have been running budget  deficits for years with lower oil price  and only now have sort of moved back  into a budget surplus uh so they're not  willing or they're not willing to  jeopardize that that to add to do  anything that collapse the price uh and  and therefore bring them back into a  budget deficit situation so it's not  surprising therefore that the opec  countries that have capacity are  unwilling to turn that tap on at this  stage we of course have the announcement  from the u.s that they're going to tap  into strategic reserves  and that's can but but really that's not  going to make much difference as you see  in the oil price it's still holding well  above 100 because the strategic reserve  released in the u.s only covers the  amount of oil that the us is now banning  from from russia approximately a million  dollars or so a million barrels a day so  it doesn't really it only covers that  that missing oil it doesn't really add  any extra oil into the global system so  in that sense  yesterday shouldn't disrupt the oil  price and that's what the oil market is  telling you today thank you so much  chris weaver for breaking it all down  for us from london
 

